* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈneɪneɪ/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-nene.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈneɪˌneɪ/}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪneɪ|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|ne|ne}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|nene}}

# The [[Hawaiian goose]], ''[[Branta sandvicensis]]'', which was [[designate#Verb|designated]] the [[state]] [[bird]] of [[Hawaii]] in 1957.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Janet Kear|authorlink=Janet Kear|author2=A. J. Berger|chapter=The Hawaiian Goose or Nene|title=The Hawaiian Goose: An Experiment in Conservation|location=Calton, Staffordshire|publisher={{w|T. & A. D. Poyser}}|year=1980|isbn=978-0-85661-025-7|newversion=reprinted|location2=London|publisher2=T. & A. D. Poyser|year2=2010|page2=42|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=jfzUBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA42|isbn2=978-1-4081-3758-1|passage=Ohelo ''Vaccinium reticulatum'' (and ''V. peleanum'') and kukaenene ''Coprosma ernodeoides'' {{...}} are the most important berries in the '''Nenes'''’ diet, and it is probably from such juicy fruit that much of their water intake comes.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Susan Scott|chapter=|title=Plants and Animals of Hawai‘i|location=Honolulu, Hi.|publisher={{w|Bess Press}}|year=1991|page=123|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6MvFZ1P71GQC&pg=PA123|isbn=978-0-935848-93-9|passage=Today, both wild and domestic dogs are a serious threat to Hawai‘i's native wildlife. The dogs prey on both seabirds and open country birds, especially the Hawaiian goose, '''''nēnē'''''. However, feral dogs aren't all bad, because one of their favorite foods is rats.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Marion Coste|title=Nēnē|series=Kolowalu Book|location=Honolulu, Hi.|publisher={{w|University of Hawaii Press}}|year=1993|page=20|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ZDxFk3E_taAC&pg=PA20|isbn=978-0-8248-1389-5|passage=Scientists think the '''nēnē''' descended from Canada geese that landed on Hawai‘i long before humans arrived. As they survived on the isolated islands for generation after generation, the geese gradually changed, becoming a new species. Today's '''nēnē''', unlike its water-loving ancestor, is a land bird.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard [Alan] Fortey|authorlink=Richard Fortey|title=The Earth: An Intimate History|location=London|publisher={{w|HarperCollins}}|year=2004|isbn=978-0-00-257011-4|location2=London|publisher2={{w|Folio Society}}|year2=2011|oclc2=800741290|page2=37|passage=The '''''nene''''' is a handsome bird that almost became extinct in the wild but was reintroduced successfully from ones bred in captivity.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Sara Benson|chapter=Haleakala National Park|title=Maui: Must-do Hikes for Everyone|series=Top Trails|location=Birmingham, Ala.|publisher={{w|Wilderness Press}}|year=2011|page=197|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=zW9KAQAAQBAJ&pg=PA197|isbn=978-0-89997-625-9|passage=Among the iconic flora and fauna found here are {{...}} the endangered '''nene''' (Hawaiian goose). After almost going extinct, '''nene''' were reintroduced into the national park in 1962 when Boy Scouts carried geese that had been raised in captivity down into Haleakala volcano in their backpacks.}}

